.Dd October 19, 2026
.Dt BSDCRYPTO 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm BSDCrypto
.Nd capability-descriptor cryptography broker
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon is the capability system's cryptography broker.
It is a socket-free capability-plane service: it exposes the well-known name
.Dq Li system.Crypto
and serves each client on its own
.Xr mac_capability 4
channel.
It mints kernel-backed crypto session
.Em descriptors
on request.
The actual cryptographic work runs through the returned
.Dv DTYPE_CRYPTO
descriptor, so no plaintext key material is ever materialized in userspace:
keys are minted inside the kernel behind the descriptor, and
.Nm
scrubs any public outputs with
.Xr explicit_bzero 3
before replying.
.Pp
.Nm
is launched and supervised by
.Xr switchboard 8
from the
.Pa /Capabilities/System/Crypto.cap
bundle; it is not started by
.Xr init 8 ,
and it is not intended to be run from the command line.
.Pp
The kernel side is provided by the
.Xr crypto 4
.Pq Pa /dev/crypto
device from the
.Sy cryptodev
module.
It must be built into the kernel or authenticated and loaded at boot.
The provider does not open the device or load the module itself.
Capsule supplies a bounded control descriptor to the verified Core recipient
selected by the boot policy's
.Va crypto_recipient
field.
.Ss Operations
A request names one operation over the client channel:
.Bl -tag -width ".Cm GENERATE_KEY"
.It Cm GENERATE
Mint a symmetric or AEAD cipher and/or HMAC session and return its descriptor.
.It Cm GENERATE_KEY
Mint an asymmetric keypair
.Pq X25519 for key exchange, or Ed25519 for signing
and return its descriptor together with the public key.
.It Cm CREATE
Create an owner-scoped kernel-resident named key.
.It Cm LEASE
Return a session descriptor for an existing named key.
.It Cm ROTATE
Advance a named key to a new generation.
.It Cm DELETE
Remove a named key.
.It Cm NAMED_LIST
Enumerate the caller's named keys, returning each key's name, generation, and
rights.
The walk is resolved on the caller's own owner scope, never a wire-supplied
owner, so one label can only ever see its own keys.
The request carries a
.Va cursor
and the reply a next cursor, so more keys than fit one reply are paged; an
out-of-range cursor is an empty page.
No descriptor is minted, no key is mutated, and no key material leaves the
kernel.
.It Cm NAMED_STAT
Resolve one of the caller's named keys by name and return its metadata
.Pq generation and rights ,
on the caller's own owner scope.
No descriptor is minted and no key material leaves the kernel.
.It Cm DIGEST
Compute a one-shot cryptographic hash of the supplied data and return the
digest.
.It Cm RANDOM
Return up to
.Dv CRYPTOCMP_MAX_RANDOM_BYTES
bytes of cryptographic random data.
.El
.Pp
Named keys are
.Em owner-scoped
by the caller's unforgeable channel label: one label can never name, lease,
rotate, or delete another label's keys.
.Ss Parameter policy
All request parameters are validated server-side before any descriptor is
minted.
The policy enforces cipher and MAC key lengths, exact IV and tag lengths per
algorithm, AEAD cipher and MAC pairing, allowed capability rights per
algorithm, an optional NIST-approved-profile gate, a bounded time-to-live, and
a restricted character set for named-key names.
An out-of-policy request is rejected without reaching the kernel.
.Ss Worker isolation
The long-lived accept parent derives an independent control descriptor for
each authenticated owner before creating a client worker with
.Xr pdfork 2 .
The kernel fixes the descriptor's owner and operation rights at derivation.
Direct named-key ioctls for another owner are rejected, including from UID zero.
An empty owner has no named-key authority.
Derived controls cannot derive additional control authority.
.Pp
The supervisor control is non-transferable, locked close-on-exec and locked
close-on-fork, and its ioctl allowlist permits only control derivation.
It does not survive into workers or the cleanup child.
A worker locks its derived control against further inheritance and transfer,
limits it to
.Dv CAP_IOCTL
and the child operation allowlist, and enters the existing worker sandbox.
The kernel owner and rights checks remain necessary even if the worker's
request dispatcher is compromised.
.Pp
The factory requires the checked
.Dq Li crypto-control
bootstrap descriptor of type
.Dq Li cryptoctl .
Missing or invalid delivery prevents startup; there is no device-open fallback.
PID 1 acquires the control, and SwitchBoard delivers it only to a verified
system Core unit whose exact label is selected by the boot policy.
The shipped Crypto unit uses the Core management class; runtime management
requests cannot stop or replace it, including requests from root.
User identity does not grant that authority.
Supervisor compromise, outstanding-handle revocation and restart semantics
remain separate security boundaries.
The process limit includes every process with the same real UID, so its
configured allowance must include other system daemons.
.Ss Installation cleanup
A separate reconciliation child receives a derived control limited to
owner enumeration, named-key listing and deletion.
It observes the delivered installation and running-bundle directories through
.Xr capreclaim 3
and deletes keys for owners judged absent by that reconciliation policy.
It cannot mint or lease keys.
Missing installation authority prevents cleanup.
This periodic reconciliation is not an acknowledged installation-removal
transaction; atomic removal, interrupted cleanup and authoritative installation
identity across upgrades and rollback remain unqualified.
Named keys are kernel-resident and do not establish persistent storage across
reboot.
.Ss Protocol
Sessions speak the FIDL library
.Sy bsd.crypto.v1
.Pq Pa /usr/share/fidl/cryptocmp.fidl
over native capability channels; see
.Xr fidl 7 .
A session must first negotiate the wire version.
A method before negotiation, an unknown method, a malformed, truncated or
oversized frame, a pre-FIDL record or an attached descriptor ends the session
without a reply and is audited as
.Dq malformed-request .
A delivered descriptor that cannot be hardened is never sent; the method fails
instead.
.Ss Startup diagnostics
Before logging is reachable, the supervisor's exit status identifies the
failed startup step: 10 (service channel), 11 (cryptodev extension),
12 (device directory), 13 (control device), 14 (descriptor restrictions),
15 (cleanup handler), 16 (provider creation), 17 (authorization),
18 (process restrictions), 19 (endpoint), 20 (capability mode), or
21 (readiness).
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width CRYPTO_RECLAIM_INTERVAL
.It Ev CRYPTO_RECLAIM_INTERVAL
Seconds between the timer passes of the key reconcile
.Pq which drop the kernel keys of bundles no longer installed or running ,
and therefore the grace window an uninstalled bundle is given before its keys
are dropped.
Default 300; values outside 10 to 86400 are ignored.
Set through the unit manifest's
.Va environment .
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width ".Pa /Capabilities/System/Crypto.cap" -compact
.It Pa /dev/crypto
kernel crypto control device
.It Pa /Capabilities/System/Crypto.cap
capability bundle shipping
.Nm
.El
.Sh DTRACE PROBES
The
.Dq crypto
provider exports
.Sy named-list ,
which fires when a
.Cm NAMED_LIST
page is produced with the caller's owner scope, the number of entries returned,
and the result.
The result argument is zero on success or an
.Xr errno 2
value on failure.
Key material and key names are never probe arguments.
.Sh LIFECYCLE
Live client workers decline idle shutdown.
Explicit stop and controlled failure exits terminate and reap tracked client
workers and the key-reclaim child before successful shutdown acknowledgement.
Startup failures retain their diagnostic exit codes while releasing resources.
Already delivered key descriptors retain their kernel-defined lifetime and
rights; stopping a worker does not revoke them.
Abrupt parent crashes and uncatchable signals require separate supervisor
recovery and are not covered by controlled-exit cleanup.
.Ss Client admission limits
At most 64 concurrent workers may serve one authenticated client label.
An excess connection is closed before allocating session resources.
Capacity returns when the provider removes the closed session or exited worker.
Existing sessions are preserved; clients must handle connection failure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cap_enter 2 ,
.Xr pdfork 2 ,
.Xr explicit_bzero 3 ,
.Xr crypto 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr fidl 7 ,
.Xr BSDExtension 8 ,
.Xr switchboard 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
daemon appeared in
.Fx 5BSD
as the capability-descriptor cryptography broker.
